[88.846782, "o", "2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults\r\nLopinavir and ritonavir can be given in once daily or twice daily dosing regimen at dosages noted in Tables 1 and 2. Lopinavir and ritonavir once daily dosing regimen is not recommended in:\r\n- Adult patients with three or more of the following lopinavir resistance-associated substitutions: L10F/I/R/V, K20M/N/R, L24I, L33F, M36I, I47V, G48V, I54L/T/V, V82A/C/F/S/T, and I84V\r\nAdult patients with three or more of the following lopinavir resistance-associated substitutions: L10F/I/R/V, K20M/N/R, L24I, L33F, M36I, I47V, G48V, I54L/T/V, V82A/C/F/S/T, and I84V .\r\n- In combination with carbamazepine, phenobarbital, or phenytoin\r\nIn combination with carbamazepine, phenobarbital, or phenytoin .\r\n- In combination with efavirenz, nevirapine, or nelfinavir\r\nIn combination with efavirenz, nevirapine, or nelfinavir .\r\n- In pediatric patients younger than 18 years of age\r\nIn pediatric patients younger than 18 years of age .\r\n- In pregnant women\r\nIn pregnant women .\r\nTable 1. Recommended Dosage in Adults – Lopinavir and Ritonavir Once Daily Regimen\r\n>>> "]
[89.364892, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][4])\n"]
[89.36518, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][4])\r\n"]
[89.365294, "o", "Recommended Dosage\r\n80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL) once daily\r\nTable 2. Recommended Dosage in Adults – Lopinavir and Ritonavir Twice Daily Regimen\r\n>>> "]
[89.862227, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][5])\n"]
[89.862398, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][5])\r\n"]
[89.862604, "o", "Recommended Dosage\r\n80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 400 mg/100 mg (5 mL) twice daily\r\nThe dose of lopinavir and ritonavir must be increased when administered in combination with efavirenz, nevirapine or nelfinavir. Table 3 outlines the dosage recommendations for twice daily dosing when lopinavir and ritonavir is taken in combination with these agents.\r\n"]
[89.862758, "o", ">>> "]
[90.369004, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][6])\n"]
[90.36925, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][6])\r\n"]
[90.369401, "o", "Recommended Dosage\r\n80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL) twice daily\r\n>>> "]
[90.858036, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][7])\n"]
[90.85831, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][7])\r\n"]
[90.858375, "o", "2.4 Dosage Recommendations in Pediatric Patients\r\nLopinavir and ritonavir oral solution is not recommended for once daily dosing in pediatric patients younger than 18 years of age. The dose of the oral solution should be administered using the calibrated cup (supplied) or oral dosing syringe.\r\nLopinavir and ritonavir oral solution is not recommended in neonates before a postmenstrual age (first day of the mother’s last menstrual period to birth plus the time elapsed after birth) of 42 weeks and a postnatal age of at least 14 days has been attained .\r\nLopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains approximately 42% (v/v) ethanol and approximately 15% (w/v) propylene glycol. Total amounts of ethanol and propylene glycol from all medicines that are to be given to pediatric patients 14 days to 6 months of age should be taken into account in order to avoid toxicity from these excipients .\r\n"]
[90.858422, "o", ">>> "]
[91.373981, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][12])\n"]
[91.374308, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][12])\r\n"]
[91.374451, "o", "2.5 Dosage Recommendations in Pregnancy\r\nAdminister 400/100 mg of lopinavir and ritonavir twice daily in pregnant patients with no documented lopinavir-associated resistance substitutions.\r\n- Once daily lopinavir and ritonavir dosing is not recommended in pregnancy\r\nOnce daily lopinavir and ritonavir dosing is not recommended in pregnancy .\r\n- There are insufficient data to recommend dosing in pregnant women with any documented lopinavir-associated resistance substitutions.\r\nThere are insufficient data to recommend dosing in pregnant women with any documented lopinavir-associated resistance substitutions.\r\n- No dosage adjustment of lopinavir and ritonavir is required for patients during the postpartum period.\r\nNo dosage adjustment of lopinavir and ritonavir is required for patients during the postpartum period.\r\n- Avoid use of lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution in pregnant women\r\nAvoid use of lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution in pregnant women .\r\n>>> "]
[99.718483, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][14])\n"]
[99.718727, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][14])\r\n"]
[99.718792, "o", "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions\r\nInitiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving lopinavir and ritonavir, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A.  Initiation of medications that inhibit or induce CYP3A may increase or decrease concentrations of lopinavir and ritonavir, respectively. These interactions may lead to:\r\n- Clinically significant adverse reactions, potentially leading to severe, life-threatening, or fatal events from greater exposures of concomitant medications.\r\n- Clinically significant adverse reactions from greater exposures of lopinavir and ritonavir.\r\n- Loss of therapeutic effect of lopinavir and ritonavir and possible development of resistance.\r\nSee Table 12 for steps to prevent or manage these possible and known significant drug interactions, including dosing recommendations . Consider the potential for drug interactions prior to and during lopinavir and ritonavir therapy; review concomitant medications during lopinavir and ritonavir therapy, and monitor for the adverse reactions associated with the concomitant medications .\r\n"]
[99.718931, "o", ">>> "]
[100.225968, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][15])\n"]
[100.226184, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][15])\r\n"]
[100.226303, "o", "5.2 Toxicity in Preterm Neonates\r\nLopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains the excipients ethanol, approximately 42% (v/v) and propylene glycol, approximately 15% (w/v). When administered concomitantly with propylene glycol, ethanol competitively inhibits the metabolism of propylene glycol, which may lead to elevated concentrations. Preterm neonates may be at increased risk of propylene glycol-associated adverse events due to diminished ability to metabolize propylene glycol, thereby leading to accumulation and potential adverse events. Postmarketing life-threatening cases of cardiac toxicity (including complete AV block, bradycardia, and cardiomyopathy), lactic acidosis, acute renal failure, CNS depression and respiratory complications leading to death have been reported, predominantly in preterm neonates receiving lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution.\r\nLopinavir and ritonavir oral solution should not be used in preterm neonates in the immediate postnatal period because of possible toxicities. A safe and effective dose of lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution in this patient population has not been established. However, if the benefit of using lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution to treat HIV infection in infants immediately after birth outweighs the potential risks, infants should be monitored closely for increases in serum osmolality and serum creatinine, and for toxicity related to lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution including: hyperosmolality, with or without lactic acidosis, renal toxicity, CNS depression (including stupor, coma, and apnea), seizures, hypotonia, cardiac arrhythmias and ECG changes, and hemolysis. Total amounts of ethanol and propylene glycol from all medicines that are to be given to infants should be taken into account in order to avoid toxicity from these excipients .\r\n"]
[100.226412, "o", ">>> "]
[100.725026, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][16])\n"]
[100.725293, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][16])\r\n5.3 Pancreatitis\r\nPancreatitis has been observed in patients receiving lopinavir and ritonavir therapy, including those who developed marked triglyceride elevations. In some cases, fatalities have been observed. Although a causal relationship to lopinavir and ritonavir has not been established, marked triglyceride elevations are a risk factor for development of pancreatitis . Patients with advanced HIV-1 disease may be at increased risk of elevated triglycerides and pancreatitis, and patients with a history of pancreatitis may be at increased risk for recurrence during lopinavir and ritonavir therapy.\r\nPancreatitis should be considered if clinical symptoms (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain) or abnormalities in laboratory values (such as increased serum lipase or amylase values) suggestive of pancreatitis occur. Patients who exhibit these signs or symptoms should be evaluated and lopinavir and ritonavir and/or other antiretroviral therapy should be suspended as clinically appropriate.\r\n"]
[100.72542, "o", ">>> "]
[101.225743, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][17])\n"]
[101.225988, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][17])\r\n"]
[101.226174, "o", "5.4 Hepatotoxicity\r\nPatients with underlying hepatitis B or C or marked elevations in transaminase prior to treatment may be at increased risk for developing or worsening of transaminase elevations or hepatic decompensation with use of lopinavir and ritonavir.\r\nThere have been postmarketing reports of hepatic dysfunction, including some fatalities. These have generally occurred in patients with advanced HIV-1 disease taking multiple concomitant medications in the setting of underlying chronic hepatitis or cirrhosis. A causal relationship with lopinavir and ritonavir therapy has not been established.\r\nElevated transaminases with or without elevated bilirubin levels have been reported in HIV-1 mono-infected and uninfected patients as early as 7 days after the initiation of lopinavir and ritonavir in conjunction with other antiretroviral agents. In some cases, the hepatic dysfunction was serious; however, a definitive causal relationship with lopinavir and ritonavir therapy has not been established.\r\nAppropriate laboratory testing should be conducted prior to initiating therapy with lopinavir and ritonavir and patients should be monitored closely during treatment. Increased AST/ALT monitoring should be considered in the patients with underlying chronic hepatitis or cirrhosis, especially during the first several months of lopinavir and ritonavir treatment .\r\n"]
[101.226327, "o", ">>> "]
[101.721534, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][18])\n"]
[101.721782, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][18])\r\n"]
[101.721941, "o", "5.5 QT Interval Prolongation\r\nPostmarketing cases of QT interval prolongation and torsade de pointes have been reported although causality of lopinavir and ritonavir could not be established. Avoid use in patients with congenital long QT syndrome, those with hypokalemia, and with other drugs that prolong the QT interval .\r\n>>> "]
[106.966708, "i", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][19])\n"]
[106.966908, "o", "print(labels[1]['chunks'][19])\r\n"]
[106.967048, "o", "5.6 PR Interval Prolongation\r\nLopinavir and ritonavir prolongs the PR interval in some patients. Cases of second or third degree atrioventricular block have been reported. Lopinavir and ritonavir should be used with caution in patients with underlying structural heart disease, pre-existing conduction system abnormalities, ischemic heart disease or cardiomyopathies, as these patients may be at increased risk for developing cardiac conduction abnormalities.\r\nThe impact on the PR interval of co-administration of lopinavir and ritonavir with other drugs that prolong the PR interval (including calcium channel blockers, beta-adrenergic blockers, digoxin and atazanavir) has not been evaluated. As a result, co-administration of lopinavir and ritonavir with these drugs should be undertaken with caution, particularly with those drugs metabolized by CYP3A. Clinical monitoring is recommended .\r\n"]
[106.967137, "o", ">>> "]
[110.902395, "i", "print(labels[0]['chunks'][14])\n"]
[110.902615, "o", "print(labels[0]['chunks'][14])\r\n"]
[110.902749, "o", "5.2\tHypersensitivity Reactions\r\nAnaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported with PAXLOVID . If signs and symptoms of a clinically significant hypersensitivity reaction or anaphylaxis occur, immediately discontinue PAXLOVID and initiate appropriate medications and/or supportive care.\r\n>>> "]
[111.405813, "i", "print(labels[0]['chunks'][15])\n"]
[111.406127, "o", "print(labels[0]['chunks'][15])\r\n"]
[111.406281, "o", "5.3\tHepatotoxicity\r\nHepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir. Therefore, caution should be exercised when administering PAXLOVID to patients with pre-existing liver diseases, liver enzyme abnormalities, or hepatitis.\r\n"]
[111.406365, "o", ">>> "]
[111.916217, "i", "print(labels[0]['chunks'][16])\n"]
[111.916423, "o", "print(labels[0]['chunks'][16])\r\n"]
[111.916555, "o", "5.4\tRisk of HIV-1 Resistance Development\r\nBecause nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir, there may be a risk of HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection .\r\n"]
[111.916743, "o", ">>> "]
[119.594622, "i", "exit()\n"]
[119.594846, "o", "exit()\r\n"]
[119.599508, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@7ddc515b1a59:/workspace# "]
[149.961916, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > generate_answers.py\nimport json\n\n# Load labels\nwith open('/workspace/data/public/labels.jsonl') as f:\n    labels = [json.loads(line) for line in f]\n\n# Helper to get the passage content\ndef get_passage(set_id, passage_id):\n    drug = next(d for d in labels if d['set_id'] == set_id)\n    idx = int(passage_id.split('_')[1])\n    return drug['chunks'][idx]\n\npaxlovid_set_id = \"8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a\"\nlop_rit_set_id = \"4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85\"\n\nanswers_data = [\n    {\n        \"qid\": \"b2531657ba5c7a78\",\n        \"status\": \"ANSWERED\",\n        \"answer\": \"Paxlovid is associated with significant drug interactions due to ritonavir, which is a strong CYP3A inhibitor. This can increase the exposure of certain concomitant medications, potentially resulting in severe, life-threatening, or fatal events. Commonly reported serious adverse reactions involved calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine) and calcium channel blockers. Conversely, drugs that induce CYP3A can decrease Paxlovid concentrations, leading to a loss of therapeutic effect and possible development of viral resistance. Co-administration with drugs highly dependent on CYP3A for clearance is contraindicated. Prior to prescribing, healthcare providers must review all medications to assess potential interactions, adjust dosages, or perform additional monitoring.\",\n        \"citations\": [\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0000\",\n                \"section\": \"WARNING: SIGNIFICANT DRUG INTERACTIONS WITH PAXLOVID\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0013\",\n                \"section\": \"5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"The most commonly reported concomitant medications resulting in serious adverse reactions were calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine), followed by calcium channel blockers.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0019\",\n                \"section\": \"7.1 Potential for PAXLOVID to Affect Other Drugs\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Co-administration of PAXLOVID with drugs highly dependent on CYP3A for clearance and for which elevated plasma concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening events is contraindicated\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0020\",\n                \"section\": \"7.2 Potential for Other Drugs to Affect PAXLOVID\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"drugs that induce CYP3A may decrease nirmatrelvir and ritonavir plasma concentrations and reduce PAXLOVID therapeutic effect\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"structured_fields\": {}\n    },\n    {\n        \"qid\": \"6f12607a1ba14dd5\",\n        \"status\": \"ANSWERED\",\n        \"answer\": \"Medications that should be avoided or are contraindicated with Paxlovid include: alpha 1-adrenoreceptor antagonists (alfuzosin), antianginals (ranolazine), antiarrhythmics (amiodarone, dronedarone, flecainide, propafenone, quinidine), anti-gout agents (colchicine in patients with renal/hepatic impairment), antipsychotics (lurasidone, pimozide), BPH agents (silodosin), cardiovascular agents (eplerenone, ivabradine), ergot derivatives (dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine), HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (lovastatin, simvastatin), immunosuppressants (voclosporin), microsomal triglyceride transfer protein inhibitors (lomitapide), migraine medications (eletriptan, ubrogepant), mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (finerenone), opioid antagonists (naloxegol), PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil for PAH), sedative/hypnotics (triazolam, oral midazolam), serotonin receptor a"]
[149.962181, "i", "gonists (flibanserin), and vasopressin receptor antagonists (tolvaptan). Additionally, strong CYP3A inducers (e.g., apalutamide, enzalutamide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, St. John's Wort) must be avoided, as well as other specific drugs like tamsulosin, rivaroxaban, and voriconazole.\",\n        \"citations\": [\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0012\",\n                \"section\": \"4 CONTRAINDICATIONS\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions and drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0023\",\n                \"section\": \"7.3 Established and Other Potentially Significant Drug Interactions\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Avoid concomitant use with PAXLOVID.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0031\",\n                \"section\": \"7.3 Established and Other Potentially Significant Drug Interactions\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Increased bleeding risk with rivaroxaban. Avoid concomitant use.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": paxlovid_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0038\",\n                \"section\": \"7.3 Established and Other Potentially Significant Drug Interactions\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Avoid concomitant use of voriconazole.\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"structured_fields\": {}\n    },\n    {\n        \"qid\": \"daef650b92e56473\",\n        \"status\": \"ANSWERED\",\n        \"answer\": \"Lopinavir-Ritonavir oral solution must be taken with food to enhance absorption. It is not recommended for use with polyurethane feeding tubes due to potential incompatibility, but compatible tubes such as silicone or polyvinyl chloride (PVC) feeding tubes can be used. Caregivers and patients should pay special attention to accurate dose administration, utilizing a calibrated dosing cup or oral dosing syringe to minimize the risk of accidental overdose or underdose. Step-by-step instructions include removing the bottle adapter, inserting it, firmly placing the oral syringe tip in the adapter, turning the bottle upside down, drawing up the prescribed dose, checking for accuracy, and swallowing the solution directly from the oral syringe.\",\n        \"citations\": [\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0001\",\n                \"section\": \"2.1 General Administration Recommendations\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution must be taken with food.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0002\",\n                \"section\": \"2.2 Administering Oral Solution by Feeding Tube\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Because lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains ethanol and propylene glycol, it is not recommended for use with polyurethane feeding tubes due to potential incompatibility.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0159\",\n                \"section\": \"17 PATIENT COUNSELING INFORMATION\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Advise patients and caregivers that the oral solution should be administered using the calibrated dosing cup (supplied) or oral dosing syringe.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0187\",\n                \"section\": \"1 Medication Guide and Instructions for Use\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Swallow the lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution directly from the oral syringe.\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"structured_fields\": {}\n    },\n    {\n        \"qid\": \"a0576a26f2a2eeac\",\n       "]
[149.962341, "i", " \"status\": \"ANSWERED\",\n        \"answer\": \"The recommended dosage regimen for Lopinavir-Ritonavir is as follows: In adults, it can be administered as a once-daily regimen of 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL oral solution) or a twice-daily regimen of 400 mg/100 mg (5 mL oral solution). The once-daily regimen is not recommended for patients with three or more lopinavir resistance-associated substitutions, those taking certain concomitant medications (e.g., carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, efavirenz, nevirapine, nelfinavir), pediatric patients under 18, or pregnant women. When twice-daily Lopinavir-Ritonavir is co-administered with efavirenz, nevirapine, or nelfinavir, the dose must be increased to 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL). In pregnant women with no lopinavir-associated resistance, the recommended dose is 400 mg/100 mg twice daily; once-daily dosing and the oral solution formulation should be avoided in pregnancy. In pediatric patients, once-daily dosing is not recommended, and dosing should be calculated based on weight or BSA to avoid underdosing. It is also not recommended in neonates before a postmenstrual age of 42 weeks and postnatal age of 14 days has been attained.\",\n        \"citations\": [\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0003\",\n                \"section\": \"2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Lopinavir and ritonavir once daily dosing regimen is not recommended in:\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0004\",\n                \"section\": \"2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL) once daily\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0005\",\n                \"section\": \"2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 400 mg/100 mg (5 mL) twice daily\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0006\",\n                \"section\": \"2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL) twice daily\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0007\",\n                \"section\": \"2.4 Dosage Recommendations in Pediatric Patients\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution is not recommended in neonates before a postmenstrual age (first day of the mother’s last menstrual period to birth plus the time elapsed after birth) of 42 weeks and a postnatal age of at least 14 days has been attained .\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0012\",\n                \"section\": \"2.5 Dosage Recommendations in Pregnancy\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Administer 400/100 mg of lopinavir and ritonavir twice daily in pregnant patients with no documented lopinavir-associated resistance substitutions.\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"structured_fields\": {}\n    },\n    {\n        \"qid\": \"9be5515bc3064979\",\n        \"status\": \"ANSWERED\",\n        \"answer\": \"The safety risks listed for Lopinavir-Ritonavir include: 1) Risk of serious, life-threatening, or fatal adverse reactions due to drug interactions with medications metabolized by CYP3A. 2) Toxicity in preterm neonates, including life-threatening cardiac toxicity (AV block, bradycardia, cardiomyopathy), lactic acidosis, renal failure, CNS depression, and respiratory complications due to the ethanol and propylene glycol excipients. 3) Pancreatitis, which has been observed with marked triglyceride elevations, occasionally leading to fatalities. 4) Hepatotoxicity, with reports of hepatic dysfunction, transaminase elevations, or hepatic decompensation, particularly in patients with underlying hepatitis B or C. 5) "]
[149.962414, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > generate_answers.py\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[149.962434, "i", "QT interval prolongation and torsade de pointes. 6) PR interval prolongation and cardiac conduction abnormalities, including second or third-degree AV block. Other risks include diabetes mellitus/hyperglycemia, immune reconstitution syndrome, lipid elevations, fat redistribution, and increased bleeding in hemophilia patients.\",\n        \"citations\": [\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0014\",\n                \"section\": \"5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Clinically significant adverse reactions, potentially leading to severe, life-threatening, or fatal events from greater exposures of concomitant medications.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0015\",\n                \"section\": \"5.2 Toxicity in Preterm Neonates\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Postmarketing life-threatening cases of cardiac toxicity (including complete AV block, bradycardia, and cardiomyopathy), lactic acidosis, acute renal failure, CNS depression and respiratory complications leading to death have been reported, predominantly in preterm neonates receiving lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0016\",\n                \"section\": \"5.3 Pancreatitis\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Pancreatitis has been observed in patients receiving lopinavir and ritonavir therapy, including those who developed marked triglyceride elevations.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0017\",\n                \"section\": \"5.4 Hepatotoxicity\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"There have been postmarketing reports of hepatic dysfunction, including some fatalities. These have generally occurred in patients with advanced HIV-1 disease taking multiple concomitant medications in the setting of underlying chronic hepatitis or cirrhosis.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0018\",\n                \"section\": \"5.5 QT Interval Prolongation\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Postmarketing cases of QT interval prolongation and torsade de pointes have been reported although causality of lopinavir and ritonavir could not be established.\"\n            },\n            {\n                \"set_id\": lop_rit_set_id,\n                \"passage_id\": \"PASSAGE_0019\",\n                \"section\": \"5.6 PR Interval Prolongation\",\n                \"supporting_quote\": \"Lopinavir and ritonavir prolongs the PR interval in some patients. Cases of second or third degree atrioventricular block have been reported.\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"structured_fields\": {}\n    },\n    {\n        \"qid\": \"18078efe9380c8dc\",\n        \"status\": \"ANSWERED\",\n        \"answer\": \"Important warnings and precautions associated with Lopinavir-Ritonavir include: 1) Risk of serious and potentially fatal adverse reactions due to CYP3A-mediated drug interactions. 2) Toxicity in preterm neonates caused by ethanol and propylene glycol excipients in the oral solution, which has resulted in fatal cardiac toxicity, renal failure, and respiratory complications. 3) Pancreatitis (sometimes fatal), especially in patients with marked triglyceride elevations. 4) Hepatotoxicity, including transaminase elevations and hepatic dysfunction, particularly in patients with underlying chronic hepatitis B or C or cirrhosis. 5) QT interval prolongation"]
[149.962521, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> import json\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\rsin receptor "]
[149.962617, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> # Load labels\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[149.963099, "o", "with open('/workspace/data/public/labels.jsonl') as f:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[149.963394, "o", "    labels = [json.loads(line) for line in f]\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> # Helper to get the passage content\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> def get_passage(set_id, passage_id):\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>     drug = next(d for d in labels if d['set_id'] == set_id)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>     idx = int(passage_id.split('_')[1])\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>     return drug['chunks'][idx]\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\rhenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, St. John's Wort) must be avoided, as well as other specific drugs like tamsulosin, rivaroxaban, and voriconazole.\",\r\n        \"cita"]
